{"kind":"request","text":"Implement the remaining P1 confidentiality/replay feasibility package and complete proposed Noseq MLS/Nostr profile, covering G1–G5 evidence, authenticated archive/recovery, owner admission, wire, bounded relay policies and strict pending-review gates.","body":{"branch":"request/protocol-feasibility","conditions":"Deliver the remaining P1 feasibility implementation and a complete proposed protocol decision, using a fresh isolated linked worktree under /Users/hughpyle/play/noseq-worktrees from current clean main c254887d4cb365bbc7ce6a8fe8b1959e34328be5 (or coordinate any later base before starting). Claim this request as noseq-builder. Read Plan 001 P1, resource budgets, acceptance/STOP conditions and current docs/crypto-feasibility.md in full. The current P1b result is a lower-API viability result, not permission to infer the remaining gates. This request commissions development implementation, complete reviewed decision proposals and evidence readiness, not production profile adoption or infrastructure deployment. Root will handle the actual adoption act and subsequent dependent requests after review.\n\nKeep the exact lower MLS/API/provider pins as the initial investigation input. Do not upgrade or replace crypto implicitly; pin and explain every actual source/package/lock/provider/wire identity and retain license attribution. Use real crypto and independently authenticated synthetic actors. Do not substitute acceptance-all authentication, an unrotated shared group key, a timer/frozen callback, or a client-local skip. The full profile may refine provisional P1b fixture domains/encodings where justified, but must identify those as experiment-to-proposed-profile changes, with new versioned fixtures and retained P1b compatibility evidence. Do not claim Marmot convergence, current Marmot proof IDs or Marmot-client interoperability. Preserve P0/P1a/P1b source-bound required evidence and historical failed runs; any obsolete experimental case needs an explicit reviewed disposition, not silent deletion.\n\nWork in this order so the recovery and admission decisions constrain the rest of the protocol:\n1. Specify the proposed wire and security model sufficiently to run real end-to-end feasibility vectors. Write docs/protocol-v0.md, docs/confidentiality-and-recovery.md and docs/relay-profile.md, plus a machine-readable exact profile/decision manifest. Keep status proposed/pending adoption explicit. Name MLS RFC version, ciphersuite, supported credentials/account-to-leaf binding, local signer operations and experimental domains/kinds/tags, creator/genesis/instance and immutable definition binding, crypto controls/application envelopes/Welcome, action author proof, authenticated sequencer order, deduplication, admission version and replay outcomes. Every code path must use those exact pinned bytes/limits. Do not infer canonicality from JavaScript JSON objects or assume an opaque server authenticates plaintext transitions.\n2. G2 is the critical next discriminating result. Decide full newcomer history versus a precisely delimited interval and state the invite/recovery promise exactly. Specify and exercise archives of original authenticated actions and their complete definition/author/protocol verification closure, authenticated coverage of a named prefix, root trust, retention, export/import, key custody/rotation and all-device-loss recovery. Separate validating the canonical signed event sequence from obtaining authenticated decryption material. An archive containing valid action signatures plus a tip hash does not by itself prove those plaintext actions correspond to the ordered ciphertext: demonstrate the cryptographic opening/retained verification closure, or explicitly select and label a trusted boundary/signer and any historically unverified interval. A snapshot must not silently stand in for verified prior history. Test tampered/truncated/wrong-instance/wrong-definition/wrong-root/coverage-hole archives, missing or corrupt keys, a fresh device, owner recovery and all-device loss using only the declared exported recovery material. Nostr identity backup alone is not an MLS/archive backup. Test recovery-key rotation and what a removed member or holder of old recovery material can and cannot read afterward. Identify exactly which retained secrets weaken forward secrecy and which service/client actors can retain them. Keep all key material synthetic; no real user keys or external service secrets.\n3. Complete G1's remaining real-client vectors using that history policy: same signed prefix in different batches, ordinary and competing Commits, sender winner/loser echo, pending application interleaving, late/stale old-epoch controls and applications, missing dependencies versus locally missing/corrupt keys, duplicate input, observed equivocation and client offline through membership changes beyond the MLS retained-epoch window. Demonstrate stable finalized outcomes under later accepted prefix extensions. Explain in the new profile why Marmot witnesses/branch withdrawal are not its accepted protocol inputs rather than dropping valid input under a claimed Marmot binding. Reconstruct accepted/pending/last-good/outcome publication state at every named boundary; accurately distinguish serialization reconstruction from actual process/browser persistence crashes. P6 still owns durable client storage/integration, but do not claim atomic persistence or recovery that this probe does not exercise. Preserve the actual removal Commit, terminal removedFromGroup handling, direct future ciphertext failure and retained-member catch-up positive control from P1B-01.\n4. G3 must bind owner-signed public transport admission and encrypted MLS transition at one exact common log boundary. Define permitted control proposers, account/device identities, admission versions, join/removal/epoch relation, only-one-half arrival, and how clients validate that decrypted membership exactly corresponds to the owner's declared change. Exercise owner offline, concurrent joins/removals, an unapproved proposer, substitution/mismatched halves, restart between halves, late old-epoch actions, revoked submitter exact retry of an already committed submission and multi-device behavior. AUTH must confer neither membership nor access to decryption keys. Distinguish no-new-action authority from retrieval of an already committed receipt. No unfinished delegated-device branch draft is normative interop. The v0 online-owner flow and explicitly capability-gated device paths are allowed; document limits honestly.\n5. Choose the availability/disposition policy. A malicious admitted member can place invalid/undecryptable content in an opaque journal; if the safe prototype result is an explicit instance stall, say so and test preservation of the last-good frontier. Do not invent a repair/skip protocol merely to make progress. Define and exercise separately: commonly provable invalid protocol/control, temporary missing dependency, local absent/corrupt secret, business refusal and runtime failure. No local decryption failure is evidence permitting a client-specific skip, and no client may use different private facts to finalize different dispositions at the same position. Local one-pending-operation serialization is allowed if clearly bounded and recovery-safe; peers must still process earlier accepted order. State the limits of observed fork detection versus unseen forks, withholding and stale-but-valid tips.\n6. Complete G4 with strict byte-level positive/negative fixtures: all Nostr signed-event fields, signed inner action/account proof closure, exact ciphertext submission identity, logical inner action identity and sequencer/relay event identity; canonical JSON/byte handling, duplicate and unknown-field policy, primitive/number/base64/length constraints, epoch and instance/genesis/definition/domain binding, expiry and signer-response substitution, retries/re-encryption. Check the current pinned NIP registry with primary sources before assigning numeric kinds. Do not apply the old Marmot v1 arbitrary-digest requirement to ordinary Nostr proof signing. Exercise local signer as the default and NIP-07/NIP-46 capability adapters; unsupported optional paths must fail clearly without breaking the local path. Label simulated extension/remote-signer capability tests as such; no claim of live external signer interoperability without actually testing it. Check supported methods rather than browser-extension presence.\n7. Freeze a coherent versioned resource budget against actual encoded crypto expansion for legitimate controls and 32 KiB actions plus encrypted definition/asset chunks. Start from Plan 001's 128 KiB event, journal/outbox/connection/filter/page/queue/fetch/replication/ingress targets; any adjustment needs a recorded reason and reviewed fixtures. Cover encoded framing overhead and bounds before allocation/crypto. Do not confuse NIP-11 field units, a historical NIP-44 limit or strfry default event size with the chosen application profile. Record group/device/KeyPackage limits if required for legitimate Commit/Welcome sizes.\n8. G5 here approves a bounded proposed NIP-01/NIP-42/NIP-11 profile and executable conformance fixtures; P5 remains responsible for full actual primary/mirror implementation and availability proof. Define normal retained history kinds, addressable discovery separately, AUTH publish/read policy, private history AND live reader ACLs, truthful advertised limits, pagination at a fixed signed tip, deduplication, retrieval completeness, retention refusal versus eviction, replication authorization and precisely what each primary/mirror operator or reader can see. Evaluate NIP-70 only with its author-AUTH/third-party replication tradeoff; it is not a membership or history ACL. Inspect the actual pinned independent mirror implementation and run a bounded local real acceptance probe for encoded event/frame sizes if available. If the mirror cannot provide the chosen read boundary or required local probe dependencies are unavailable, report the exact limitation and leave G5 pending; do not substitute a fake mirror or deploy a cloud service. A URL, relay OK, EOSE or an advertised profile is not by itself complete retained recovery evidence.\n9. Produce the full adversary-by-asset table required in Plan 001: network observer; primary/mirror reader/operator; malicious relay/provider; compromised generic host; removed member; admitted malicious member; stolen device; leaked recovery archive. For action content, confidential definitions/assets, identity, membership, instance existence, traffic timing and sizes, give visible/hidden/bounded meaning, mechanism, residual exposure and fixture or a precise explicitly untested limitation. A trusted-host exclusion must remain explicit: mutable malicious UI can read client secrets.\n\nUse real Node and actual Chromium for client crypto/recovery/admission/wire feasibility; use actual local workerd/relay only where that target's behavior is claimed. Factor small development-only modules or the plan's isolated src/crypto/adapter.ts as needed, but do not integrate production behavior into the generic host, Worker sequencer, relay, app UI or onboarding before full P1 acceptance and a separately reviewed/adopted profile. Prefer a straightforward bounded implementation over a general framework for hypothetical profiles.\n\nImplement scripts/protocol-gates.ts and the necessary strict evidence runner/verifier with exact source/tree/lock/runtime/profile/library/fixture/frontier/definition identities, required cases, retained raw evidence and successful prerequisites. A separately named candidate probe may succeed when the defined observations reproduce. test:crypto-feasibility and gate:protocol may exit 0 only when all complete G1–G5 requirements AND actual reviewed decision records validate; until then report unpassed/pending with nonzero status. Avoid circular or fabricated review approvals: implement and document a two-phase evidence closure, using real detached review/decision records bound to immutable profile content if needed. An author-controlled boolean or invented approval ID is not a reviewed decision record. Preserve prior successful and failed attempts, reject missing/skipped/corrupted/duplicate/foreign-source evidence, and do not make P0's reserved-stage checks green by removing required coverage. If final command activation needs a small follow-up after real profile approval/adoption, keep it explicitly pending in this package and explain the exact remaining step.\n\nAllowed scope: the three named protocol/security/relay documents, docs/crypto-feasibility.md and docs/toolchain.md, development crypto adapter/support modules, feasibility fixtures/tests, protocol gate/evidence scripts and dedicated configs, exact package/lock changes required for those probes, NOTICE and ignored-output rules. Small CI additions may exercise bounded probes without weakening the existing baseline. Do not edit notes/**, plans/** or README.md; separate noseq-atseq assessment retains that ownership. No Atseq source changes, real key collection, upstream publication, paid/cloud deployment, broad machine configuration or P2–P9 production implementation.\n\nBound this continuation to two engineering days before a clear interim complete/continue/change/stop report, and raise material impossibility/dependency failures earlier. Do not hide a failed confidentiality or recovery claim behind a narrower test. Obtain thorough independent exact-head review from noseq-reviewer across all changed paths, gate completeness, source fidelity, evidence rejection and the proposed protocol decision. Retain findings, correct within this live request, and request fresh review after any candidate change. Requester-ratify the actual verdict and coordinate a guarded landing only after approval. Review under this request can approve the proposal and evidence readiness; it does not adopt production encryption or authorize dependent stages. Root owns origin source/sequence sync, watched-file publication and the subsequent explicit profile adoption/completion decision.","target_ref":"refs/heads/main","to":"981184b7467c74c2c8cd2783cb8bbc857427c7592a16425d0e2b7206a3768515"}}